# 8. SCROLL-DRIVEN CINEMATIC CAMERA

This is one of the most important engineering features.

The camera should move through predetermined, art-directed compositions as the user scrolls.

Do not implement uncontrolled free-roaming camera navigation as the default.

### Required behaviour

- Define camera keyframes for each major homepage scene.
- Interpolate camera position, orientation and target smoothly.
- Synchronise scene changes with section progress.
- Use easing where appropriate.
- Maintain clear text composition.
- Avoid extreme rotations.
- Avoid camera clipping.
- Ensure transitions are reversible when scrolling upward.
- Handle fast scrolling and direct anchor navigation correctly.
- Prevent camera movement from interfering with reading.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

The camera should support the storytelling, not compete with it.

Use a single source of truth for scene progress to avoid conflicting animation systems.

Do not hijack native scrolling.

Do not require continuous scroll wheel input merely to move between pages.

The portfolio must retain normal browser scrolling, links and history behaviour.

